public String issueCode(UUID requestId) {
  String sendsKey = KEY_PREFIX+"sends:"+requestId;
  String resendKey = KEY_PREFIX+"resend:"+requestId;
  // resend interval
  Boolean allowed = redis.opsForValue().setIfAbsent(resendKey,"1",props.verification().resendInterval());
  if (Boolean.FALSE.equals(allowed)) throw ApiException.tooManyRequests("...");
  Long sends = redis.opsForValue().increment(sendsKey);
  if (sends != null && sends == 1) redis.expire(sendsKey, window);
  if (sends != null && sends > maxSends) throw ...
  ...
}
```
Careful: if send limit exceeded after increment, resend key set — minor. Check count before? Order: check resend interval first then increment. When exceeds, we've consumed interval; acceptable but better check via get before increment:

```java
Long current = parse(redis.opsForValue().get(sendsKey));
if (current >= maxSends) throw 429;
```
Then setIfAbsent resend; then increment. Race conditions minor; Redis INCR atomic. Use increment then if > max → throw (counter stays 4; further blocked). Fine, but interval key set even on failure. Reorder: increment first; if >max throw; then setIfAbsent resend → if false, decrement back? Complicated. Use Lua? Overkill. Simplicity: check current count, then resend key, then increment.

